trace where a long present waits

The in-game menu still missed latches on 0045: 142 in two minutes of
Donkey Kong Country, every one of them with the menu up. Most of its long
presents spent 13.5 or 16.2 ms in `advance` with the core paused -- input,
the feed and the app's update, normally 0.1 ms -- and advance, render and
swap summed to two blanks every time. That reads as waiting, not work, but
the trace could not say on what: two theories in a row were argued from
these numbers and only one of them held.

Measurement only, behind SLOT_TRACE_LATENCY like the rest of the trace. A
long present now also says:

- how `advance` split between the input poll, the feed and the update;
- of the present's wall time, how long the draw was on a CPU, how long it
  sat in a run queue waiting for one, and what is left -- blocked or
  asleep, the swap's wait for the blank included -- from the thread's
  `/proc/thread-self/schedstat`. Where the kernel has no schedstat it says
  so instead.

A run queue wait is another thread holding the draw's CPU; blocked is a
lock, a channel or a sleep, which on a CONFIG_HZ 100 kernel can return a
tick late.

Test: schedstat counts 20 ms of spinning as at least 10 ms on a CPU.

Applies on top of 0045, upstream 91499a9.

diff --git a/crates/slot/src/niceness.rs b/crates/slot/src/niceness.rs
